## Going back to work

Many people go back to work within a few weeks to a few months of a heart attack or treatment, depending on their recovery and their job. Your GP can give you a **fit note** that suggests changes, such as a **phased return**.

## Your rights

Coronary heart disease can count as a disability under the **Equality Act 2010** if it has a **substantial, long-term effect** on your daily life, judged as if you were not taking treatment. If so, your employer must consider **reasonable adjustments**, such as:

- a **phased return** or reduced hours
- **lighter duties**, or less heavy lifting
- **flexible hours** and time off for appointments and cardiac rehab
- a **parking space** near the entrance

**Access to Work** can help pay for support or travel.

## Benefits

PIP is based on how your condition affects you on **most days**. If angina, breathlessness or heart failure limit you, they may affect activities such as:

- **moving around:** how far you can walk before chest pain or breathlessness stops you, reliably and repeatedly
- **preparing food** and **washing:** if standing, lifting or effort brings on symptoms

{% checker tool="pip" /%}

If you are over State Pension age, look at [Attendance Allowance](/benefits/attendance-allowance/). If you cannot work because of your health, you may be able to get **[Universal Credit](/benefits/universal-credit-health/)** or **[New Style Employment and Support Allowance](/benefits/esa/)**.

## Prescriptions

Heart disease does not give you free prescriptions in England, but a **prescription prepayment certificate** can save money if you need several items. Prescriptions are **free in Wales**, and in England they are free for everyone aged 60 or over.
